{"type":"text","timestamp":1788998446869,"sessionID":"ses_f77642085ffeC3WrcH4De1u9FV","part":{"id":"prt_0889ddb1b001623GHFhoYf7yWA","messageID":"msg_0889dd732001HC8T1VRshuVZcU","sessionID":"ses_f77642085ffeC3WrcH4De1u9FV","type":"text","text":"Fixed. The bug: `switchcheck` was treating a `term`-typed subject the same as any other \"open\" domain — closable only by a bare wildcard/name arm, else flagged `switch_inexhaustive`. But `term` is unboundedly heterogeneous (any shape/depth), so the checker can never compute a residual for it at all, unlike `atom` (flat, still trackable as open-but-closable). Per c11 (`Bad(e) -> e switch { (:ok, v) => w, (:no, w) => w }`, where `e: term`), the reference only reports `unbound_variable` — no exhaustiveness verdict.\n\nFix: when the subject's base kind is `term`, exhaustiveness (and the shared reachability machinery) is skipped entirely for that switch, rather than guessing. All other diagnostics (rebinding, unbound_variable, vacuous_arm, etc.) are unaffected.\n\nAll 13 local cases now match their directory-name hints:\n- c01/c02/c05: well-formed → no output\n- c03/c06: `switch_inexhaustive`\n- c04: `unreachable_arm`\n- c07: `rebinding` (bare `n` rebinds the parameter — the dir name \"guarded\" is about the guard's *presence*, not that a guard makes it legal)\n- c08: `return_not_declared`\n- c09: `arg_not_accepted`\n- c10: `switch_in_guard`\n- c11: `unbound_variable` only\n- c12: `vacuous_arm`\n- c13: `unsatisfiable_arm_guard`","time":{"start":1788998441755,"end":1788998446840}}}
